
## The layers

- **The structure is declared in `.config/rotproof.toml`, and `rotproof check` fails when the tree differs from it,
  either way.** Delete an unused layer and list it in `absent`; to bring one back, remove it from `absent` and run
  `rotproof create --yes`, which makes only what is missing (without `--yes`, it lists the layers it would make and
  writes nothing). Code outside every layer fails too: move it into a layer, or
  list its path in `unchecked` (helper scripts, generated code). Code of the project that is not in a layer yet is
  listed in `moving` instead: no check reads it, and while a path is listed `rotproof check` prints how many files are
  left, which to move next and the layers each could go in (not `domain`, `protocol`, `application` or
  `presentation`, for a file that would do I/O there or import a package no list allows there, in Python and
  TypeScript). Move them into the layers in the order it gives, and choose among the layers that pass by what the code
  is for. It lists, too, every import of such a path (`unchecked` as well) by those four layers, which no check
  judges: a helper moved there is not a fix.{not_layers}
{direction}

{table}
- **`application` and `infrastructure` meet only in `protocol` (dependency inversion).** A use case implements an input
  port of `protocol.inbound` and calls the output ports of `protocol.outbound`; an entry point in
  `infrastructure.inbound` calls only the input ports, and a connection in `infrastructure.outbound` implements an
  output port. `composition` alone builds the implementations and wires them, and the entry file only calls it. Define
  a port only when it is called, with only the methods called.
- **A vocabulary of values (the kinds an entry may have) may be written twice, in `protocol.inbound` and in the unit's
  own `domain`, and no more:** a third copy is born where the values are needed at run time. So each copy builds its
  type from its run-time values (in TypeScript, `typeof KINDS[number]` from an `as const` array; a Python `Enum` and a
  Rust `enum` are one already); `protocol.inbound` gives those values, not only the type, and entry points, the UI and
  tests use them and never write a list of their own; and `application` converts between the two copies exhaustively,
  so that a kind missing on either side fails the type checker (a `switch` with an exhaustiveness check).
- **A part goes in the layer whose concept it speaks.** No layer holds general-purpose parts: a general procedure comes
  from the standard library or an external one, and wanting to write one's own is a sign of a concept not named yet.
- **An import the table forbids passes only as an approval, and an approval is asked for by the user.** Fix a
  forbidden import. Run `rotproof approve <file> <import> --reason "<why>"` only when the user asked you to keep it
  (a framework forces it, a fix cannot wait for the refactoring), and say so in your report, with the reason: it adds
  the import to `.config/rotproof-approved.toml`, signed with git's `user.name`, and asks for nothing else.
  `rotproof check` prints every approval on every run, fails on one that matches no forbidden import any more (after
  fixing an approved import, run `rotproof approve --prune`, which removes such entries), and fails when the
  approvals are more than `approved_max` in `.config/rotproof.toml` lets the project hold, if it has one. Raising
  `approved_max` is the user's decision: never add, raise or remove it to pass the check.
{units}{rules}{ui}